Z||000003||stillofknight||04-10-2000||11:51 PM|||| Hello. What exactly is it that you need to know. I can probably be of some help.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Dan86gt3.8||04-11-2000||11:04 AM||||Well you need to hook up thees wires: a constant hot wire striaght to the battery, a chasis ground (a good one) a remote turn on lead, which is a little 16-18 gauge wire/and is usualy blue, and then a pair of rca cables to go from your radio to the input rca's of your amp.  It would also help more if you said how many channels your amp has, where you are going to put it, if you have rca outputs on your radio, etc etc.  I hooked up a 2 channel that I put behind the drivers seat and it was really weasy, you just have to take of the center consloe to rout the wires.  Anyways if you can take apart interior pannels, and know how to use crimped connectors, you should be in good shape.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Homicidal||04-11-2000||03:19 PM||||2 channel, yes it has RCA outputs (it's a 99 JVC) and it's going behind the seat or under the seat, not sure whether passenger or driver.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dan86gt3.8||04-12-2000||04:12 PM||||Well what you need to do is pull the radio and the center console.  Run a pair or rca cables and the remote turn on wire, (a blue wire that shoeld be in the wiring harness that came witht he radio.  Then go back and figure where your gona run the power wire, (I opted to drill a hole and use the gromet that came in my amp instalition kit, {that was a 20 spot at radio shack} to run the wire in right behind/under the battery.  You can poke a hole in one of the gromets for the shifter cables (the top 2 if stick, the bottom one if its an auto) well anyways you can poke a hole in the one not being used, BUT that gets the power wire close to the sparkplug wires, and you can start picking up interference so I steered clear.  Anyways after that all you need is to find a good chasis ground and you are good to go.  Thts just the way I did it I found it keeps all the wires neat if you run them underneath the console.  If you have rca's and a remote turn on lead from the new radio, there is not much to it.<BR>||||reg||1
